## FFmpeg libraries (LGPL-2.1-or-later)

Soyuz uses libraries from the FFmpeg project (https://ffmpeg.org) under the GNU Lesser
General Public License, version 2.1 or later: `libavutil`, `libswresample`, `libswscale`,
`libavcodec` and `libavformat` (FFmpeg 9.0, library majors 61 / 7 / 10 / 63 / 63). They are
**shared libraries loaded at run time** (crate `soyuz-media`, `libloading`); nothing of
FFmpeg is compiled or linked into the Soyuz executables, and Soyuz keeps working without
them (videos then show metadata only). FFmpeg is a trademark of Fabrice Bellard, originator
of the FFmpeg project.

- **What is bundled.** The Windows zips, the MSI, the Linux tarball and rpm and the macOS
  app and tarball carry the five libraries built by the Soyuz project itself
  (`scripts/ffmpeg-build.sh`, pins in `third_party/ffmpeg/pins.env`) from unmodified FFmpeg
  release sources, configured **without** `--enable-gpl`, `--enable-nonfree` and
  `--enable-version3`, with dav1d (AV1 decoding), libopus and libvpx linked in statically and
  the hardware encoders of each OS through the vendors' header-only SDKs (NVIDIA
  nv-codec-headers, AMD AMF, Intel VPL; VideoToolbox on macOS, VAAPI on Linux). The Flatpak
  bundles nothing and uses the runtime's FFmpeg. The exact version, configure line and
  toolchain of the copy you have are in `BUILD.txt` next to its `LICENSE.md` (`ffmpeg/` in the
  zip and MSI, `share/licenses/soyuz/ffmpeg/` on Linux, `Contents/Resources/ffmpeg/` in the app).
- **Source code and how it was built.** The complete corresponding source (every input
  archive, with SHA-256 sums in `SOURCES.txt`), the build record and the libraries themselves
  are published on the `ffmpeg-runtime-<version>` release of the Soyuz repository
  (https://github.com/ulaas/soyuz/releases), separate from the app releases; the build script
  is in the repository. Soyuz applies no patches to FFmpeg.
- **Replacing the libraries.** As the LGPL requires, you can swap in your own build of the
  same major: put the libraries into `[media] ffmpeg_dir`, next to the Soyuz executable, or
  into `<config dir>/ffmpeg` (see the user guide, "Media"). The signed macOS app carries the
  `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` entitlement for that purpose.
- **Patents.** Soyuz and these libraries come with no patent licence. Some of the codecs
  they implement (H.264, HEVC, AAC and others) are covered by patents in some jurisdictions;
  whether you need a licence to use them depends on where you are and what you do
  (https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html). The Soyuz project cannot give legal advice.

The LGPL-2.1 text and the notices of dav1d, Opus, libvpx and the header-only SDKs are in
the appendix below.

